ards, some pairs, and that tempting straight. Looking at the history, the Landlord already burned their Red Joker early, which could be a sign of desperation or a power play. My teammate seems solid, and I need to capitalize on their strength. My cards are ♠3, ♥3, ♥4, ♠5, ♠6, ♣6, ♥7, ♣7, ♦7, ♣8, ♠9, ♦9, ♠10, ♥10, ♥J, ♥Q. I\'ve got pairs of 3s, 6s, 9s, 10s and a triplet of 7s, but no real firepower. If I play any single or pair, I risk the Landlord unleashing their high cards and gaining control. My real strength lies in the potential 10-card straight from 3 to Queen.\n\nPlaying the straight has pros and cons. It sheds 10 cards at once, which is massive. My hand becomes 3, 6, 7, 7, 9, 10. That\'s weak, yes, but it forces the Landlord to respond significantly. I hold 3,3,4,5,6,6,7,7,7,8,9,9,10,10,J,Q. I have no high value cards to play. I\'m blocking 3,4,5,6,7,8,9,10,J,Q. It will take a straight or bomb from the Landlord to beat that, and I doubt he has a long straight due to my position. The best-case scenario is that the Landlord has a bomb (likely A or K) that is forced to take the hit. If the Landlord doesn\'t bomb, I\'ve cleared a huge chunk of my hand and am at 6 cards. Teammate is strong enough to lead. My hand is so connected that holding it would guarantee failure. I\'m going to take the gamble.\n\nLet\'s check the suits. I see that the listed straight: ♠3 ♥4 ♠5 ♣6 ♥7 ♣8 ♠9 ♥10 ♥J ♥Q uses exactly one of the two suits that I have for 3, 6, 9, 10, J, and Q. The 4, 5, 8, and 7 are all unique suits.\n\nIf I play a shorter straight, it breaks up too many cards and risks letting the Landlord maintain control. I am better off with my current connected state.\n\nTherefore, the play is clear: I\'m playing the 10-card straight, no question. It\'s the best strategic move in this situation, given my lack of high cards, the Landlord\'s Red Joker play, and my teammate\'s strong play. I get to clear 10 cards with minimal risk and allow teammate to then pick up the play. The